# Engineering Rules - GrowthBae Project

This file contains the engineering standards and principles for this codebase.
ALL code generation and edits must follow these rules.

## 🏗 GENERAL PHILOSOPHY

- Write **minimal, boring, predictable code**
- Prefer **clarity over cleverness**
- Every file should do **one thing well** (Single Responsibility Principle)
- Code should be **easy to delete or extend tomorrow**
- Always ask: "If we add 3 more features next month, will this still hold up?"

## 📁 PROJECT STRUCTURE & ORGANIZATION

### Feature-Oriented, Layered Structure

```
app/                    → Next.js routes, pages, layouts
components/
  ├── ui/              → Atomic, reusable presentational components
  ├── blocks/          → Composed sections (navbar, hero, etc.)
  ├── layout/          → Layout components (header, footer)
  └── sections/        → Page-specific sections
lib/
  ├── api/             → API clients, fetchers
  ├── hooks/           → Shared custom React hooks
  ├── design/          → Design tokens, theme config
  └── utils/           → Pure utility helpers
types/                 → Shared TypeScript types & interfaces
```

### Organization Rules

1. **No business logic inside UI components**
2. **No network calls directly in components** (except server components with clear isolation)
3. Keep **cross-cutting concerns** (API, state, design, utils) in `lib/` or `types/`
4. One file = one concern
5. Colocate related code (e.g., all user-related API calls in `lib/api/users.ts`)

## 🧾 TYPESCRIPT STANDARDS

### Strictness
- ✅ Enable **strict mode** always
- ❌ Never use `any` unless explicitly justified with a comment
- ✅ Prefer `type` and `interface` over inline object annotations
- ✅ Export reusable types from `types/` or closest domain folder
- ✅ Use enums/union types for fixed sets instead of magic strings

### Type Guidelines
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
type Status = "draft" | "published" | "archived"
interface User {
  id: string
  name: string
  status: Status
}

// ❌ BAD
function doSomething(data: any) { }
const user = { name: "John" } // Missing type
```

## ⚛ REACT / NEXT.JS RULES

### Component Architecture

1. **Server Components by default** in Next.js
2. Only use `"use client"` when:
   - Using state, effects, or browser APIs
   - Handling user interactions
3. Prefer **functional components** with explicit props types
4. Keep components **small** (ideally < 150 lines)

### Logic Extraction
Extract complex logic into:
- **Custom hooks** in `lib/hooks/` (e.g., `useFeatureX`)
- **Pure helpers** in `lib/utils/`

### JSX Rules

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Clean, extracted logic
export function ProjectCard({ project }: ProjectCardProps) {
  const statusColor = getStatusColor(project.status)
  const formattedDate = formatDate(project.createdAt)

  return (
    <Card>
      <CardTitle>{project.title}</CardTitle>
      <Badge color={statusColor}>{project.status}</Badge>
      <Caption>{formattedDate}</Caption>
    </Card>
  )
}

// ❌ BAD - Inline complex logic
export function ProjectCard({ project }: ProjectCardProps) {
  return (
    <Card>
      <CardTitle>{project.title}</CardTitle>
      <Badge color={
        project.status === "draft" ? "gray" : 
        project.status === "published" ? "green" : "red"
      }>
        {project.status}
      </Badge>
    </Card>
  )
}
```

### Props Guidelines
- Props should be **explicit and typed**
- No `any` or unstructured `props`
- Use destructuring with types
- Provide sensible defaults

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
interface ButtonProps {
  variant?: "primary" | "ghost" | "underline"
  size?: "sm" | "md" | "lg"
  children: React.ReactNode
}

export function Button({ 
  variant = "primary", 
  size = "md", 
  children 
}: ButtonProps) { }

// ❌ BAD
export function Button(props: any) { }
```

## 🎨 STYLING & DESIGN SYSTEM

### GBAE Design System Rules

1. **Use Tailwind + design tokens ONLY**
   - ❌ No inline styles
   - ❌ No raw hex colors
   - ❌ No arbitrary values like `w-[347px]` unless justified

2. **All colors from design system**
   ```tsx
   // ✅ GOOD
   <div className="bg-secondary text-primary">
   <div className="bg-accent hover:bg-primary">
   
   // ❌ BAD
   <div className="bg-[#F5F1E5]">
   <div style={{ backgroundColor: '#004225' }}>
   ```

3. **Spacing follows 8px grid**
   ```tsx
   // ✅ GOOD - Multiples of 8px
   <div className="space-y-4">  {/* 32px */}
   <div className="py-8">       {/* 64px */}
   
   // ❌ BAD - Arbitrary values
   <div className="space-y-[23px]">
   <div className="py-[47px]">
   ```

4. **No one-off pixel values** unless there's a strong reason
   - If breaking this rule, add a comment explaining why

## 🧩 COMPONENT DESIGN PRINCIPLES

### Atoms (`components/ui/`)
- **Purely presentational**
- No business logic
- No data fetching
- Just layout, styling, and props
- Accept all necessary data via props

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Pure, presentational
export function Button({ variant, children, onClick }: ButtonProps) {
  return (
    <button className={cn(variantStyles[variant])} onClick={onClick}>
      {children}
    </button>
  )
}

// ❌ BAD - Has business logic
export function Button({ userId }: { userId: string }) {
  const user = fetchUser(userId) // ❌ Fetching in atom
  return <button>Hello {user.name}</button>
}
```

### Blocks/Sections (`components/blocks/`, `components/sections/`)
- Accept props for content (no hard-coded copy)
- Composable: reusable on multiple pages
- Responsible for layout of atoms, NOT data fetching
- Can accept data as props

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Accepts data via props
interface HeroProps {
  title: string
  description: string
  ctaText: string
  ctaHref: string
}

export function HeroSection({ title, description, ctaText, ctaHref }: HeroProps) {
  return (
    <Section>
      <Container>
        <H1>{title}</H1>
        <Lead>{description}</Lead>
        <Button href={ctaHref}>{ctaText}</Button>
      </Container>
    </Section>
  )
}

// ❌ BAD - Hard-coded content
export function HeroSection() {
  return (
    <Section>
      <H1>Welcome to GrowthBae</H1> {/* ❌ Hard-coded */}
    </Section>
  )
}
```

### Page-Level (`app/`)
- Orchestrate data + blocks
- Fetch data (in server components) and pass into blocks via props
- Keep thin - just composition

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Server component, fetches and composes
export default async function ProjectsPage() {
  const projects = await fetchProjects()
  
  return (
    <>
      <HeroSection 
        title="Our Projects"
        description="Explore our case studies"
      />
      <ProjectsGrid projects={projects} />
    </>
  )
}
```

### Splitting Rule
**If a component grows too big or has multiple responsibilities → SPLIT it**

Signs to split:
- File > 200 lines
- Component does more than one thing
- Multiple levels of nested conditionals
- Hard to name the component concisely

## 🌐 API & DATA LAYER

### Structure
All data fetching lives in:
- `lib/api/` for client-side fetching
- Server components for server-side fetching

### API File Organization
One file per domain:
```
lib/api/
  ├── projects.ts
  ├── case-studies.ts
  ├── users.ts
  └── analytics.ts
```

### API Function Guidelines

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Typed, clear, handles errors
export async function fetchProjects(): Promise<Project[]> {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}/projects`, {
      headers: {
        Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.API_TOKEN}`,
      },
    })
    
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Failed to fetch projects: ${response.status}`)
    }
    
    return await response.json()
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error fetching projects:', error)
    return [] // Graceful fallback
  }
}

// ❌ BAD - No types, no error handling
export async function fetchProjects() {
  const res = await fetch('https://hardcoded-url.com/projects')
  return res.json()
}
```

### Rules
1. Use **typed functions** with clear input + output types
2. Handle errors gracefully; don't crash the UI
3. Never hard-code URLs or tokens; read from env variables
4. Return fallback data or throw meaningful errors

## 🚦 STATE, ERRORS & LOADING

### Every Async View Must Have

1. **Loading state**
   ```tsx
   if (isLoading) return <Skeleton />
   ```

2. **Error state**
   ```tsx
   if (error) return <ErrorMessage>Failed to load projects</ErrorMessage>
   ```

3. **Empty state** (when applicable)
   ```tsx
   if (projects.length === 0) return <EmptyState>No projects yet</EmptyState>
   ```

### UI Rule
❌ **Do NOT leave placeholders** like `TODO: add error handling`

✅ **Always show proper UX:**
- Loading → Use skeleton or subtle loader
- Error → Simple, clear message with retry option
- Empty → Soft, helpful copy with next action

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD - Complete states
export function ProjectsList({ projects, isLoading, error }: Props) {
  if (isLoading) {
    return <ProjectsSkeleton />
  }
  
  if (error) {
    return (
      <ErrorState>
        <Body>Failed to load projects. Please try again.</Body>
        <Button onClick={retry}>Retry</Button>
      </ErrorState>
    )
  }
  
  if (projects.length === 0) {
    return (
      <EmptyState>
        <Body>No projects yet. Create your first one!</Body>
        <Button href="/projects/new">Create Project</Button>
      </EmptyState>
    )
  }
  
  return <ProjectsGrid projects={projects} />
}

// ❌ BAD - No error/empty states
export function ProjectsList({ projects }: Props) {
  return <ProjectsGrid projects={projects} />
}
```

## 🔁 EXTENSIBILITY / FUTURE-PROOFING

### Design for Tomorrow

When implementing a feature, structure as if we might:
- Add one more variant
- Add one more data source
- Add one more page using the same block

### Patterns

1. **Prefer configuration over duplication**
   ```typescript
   // ✅ GOOD - Configurable
   const FEATURE_CONFIG = [
     { icon: "🚀", title: "Fast", description: "..." },
     { icon: "🔒", title: "Secure", description: "..." },
     { icon: "📈", title: "Scalable", description: "..." },
   ]
   
   return FEATURE_CONFIG.map(feature => (
     <FeatureCard key={feature.title} {...feature} />
   ))
   
   // ❌ BAD - Duplicated JSX
   return (
     <>
       <FeatureCard icon="🚀" title="Fast" description="..." />
       <FeatureCard icon="🔒" title="Secure" description="..." />
       <FeatureCard icon="📈" title="Scalable" description="..." />
     </>
   )
   ```

2. **Use props, config objects, and mapping structures**
3. **For anything repeated 3+ times → extract**

### The "3 Times Rule"
If you copy-paste similar code 3 times, stop and extract it into:
- A reusable component
- A utility function
- A configuration object

## 📏 FILE & FUNCTION SIZE LIMITS

### Limits
- **Component files:** ≤ 200 lines (aim for ≤ 150)
- **Functions:** ≤ 40 lines (aim for ≤ 30)
- **JSX blocks:** ≤ 50 lines before extracting subcomponent

### When Breaking Limits
Consciously refactor:
1. Split into smaller helpers
2. Extract hooks (`useSomething`)
3. Extract subcomponents
4. Move logic to separate files

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Extracted logic
function useProjectFilters(projects: Project[]) {
  const [filters, setFilters] = useState<Filters>({})
  const filtered = useMemo(() => 
    applyFilters(projects, filters), 
    [projects, filters]
  )
  return { filtered, filters, setFilters }
}

export function ProjectsPage() {
  const { data: projects } = useProjects()
  const { filtered, filters, setFilters } = useProjectFilters(projects)
  
  return (
    <>
      <FilterBar filters={filters} onChange={setFilters} />
      <ProjectsGrid projects={filtered} />
    </>
  )
}

// ❌ BAD - Everything in one component (100+ lines)
export function ProjectsPage() {
  // 20 lines of filter logic
  // 30 lines of fetch logic
  // 50 lines of JSX
  // = 100+ lines total
}
```

## 🔧 TOOLING & QUALITY

### Code Quality Standards

1. **Enforce ESLint + Prettier**
   - No unused imports
   - No unreachable code
   - Consistent formatting

2. **Prefer named exports**
   ```typescript
   // ✅ GOOD
   export function Button() { }
   export function Card() { }
   
   // ⚠️ Use sparingly
   export default function HomePage() { } // Only for pages
   ```

3. **Use meaningful names**
   - ✅ Good: `ProjectCard`, `SectionWrapper`, `fetchProjects`
   - ❌ Bad: `PC`, `Wrap`, `getProj`

4. **Avoid abbreviations** unless absolutely standard
   - ✅ OK: `id`, `url`, `api`, `ui`
   - ❌ Avoid: `usr`, `proj`, `desc`, `btn`

### File Naming Conventions
- Components: `kebab-case.tsx` → `project-card.tsx`
- Utilities: `kebab-case.ts` → `format-date.ts`
- Types: `kebab-case.ts` → `user-types.ts`
- API: `kebab-case.ts` → `case-studies.ts`

## ✅ YOUR JOB AS AN AI ASSISTANT

From now on, when generating or editing code in this repo:

1. **FOLLOW all rules above**
2. **If you break a rule for a good reason, EXPLAIN IT IN A COMMENT**
3. **Always optimize for:**
   - Readability
   - Minimalism
   - Ability to extend features tomorrow with minimal refactoring

### Before Submitting Code, Check:
- [ ] No business logic in UI components?
- [ ] All colors using design tokens?
- [ ] All spacing following 8px grid?
- [ ] Loading/error/empty states handled?
- [ ] Types defined (no `any`)?
- [ ] File < 200 lines?
- [ ] Functions < 40 lines?
- [ ] Repeated code extracted?
- [ ] Meaningful names used?
- [ ] No unused imports?

### Red Flags to Avoid
- ❌ Hard-coded content in reusable components
- ❌ API calls in UI components
- ❌ Inline styles or arbitrary Tailwind values
- ❌ Files over 200 lines
- ❌ Missing TypeScript types
- ❌ No error handling
- ❌ Duplicated code (3+ times)
- ❌ Abbreviations in names

